from lesson_writer import author,CPP
author('src/org/minima/database/userprefs/UserDB.md','Gives named application preferences to the JSON database: wallet/network settings, user metadata, scripts, backup options and RPC users.',[
 'Each scalar accessor delegates to JsonDB using a stable key. Defaults are computed when read: the welcome string includes the current version, hash rate defaults to one million, and Magic preferences use protocol constants.',
 'Setters change in-memory JSON; this class does not automatically save a file after every preference change. The owner coordinates persistence.',
 'Webhooks and permanent-contact lists are converted between Java lists and JSON arrays. RPC-user and uninstalled-app getters instead return JSON arrays directly, allowing aliases to observe mutation.',
 'Uninstalled names are lowercased using the process locale and have literal ASCII spaces removed. Tabs and other whitespace remain. Adding removes one equal existing entry, then appends the normalized name; this is not a global deduplication pass over arbitrary loaded data.',
 'setSlaveNode writes enabled before host. getPublicMDS gives the global command-line enable flag precedence over the stored false value.',
 'Encrypted-seed methods merely store/retrieve MiniData. Encryption and authentication occur elsewhere; storing a field with that name does not encrypt it.'
], 'Native methods retain the same keys/defaults and reuse JsonDB, MiniUtil list conversions and Java-compatible locale-sensitive lowercasing. Shared JSON-array ownership preserves important aliases; optional strings support null parameters, while UTF-16 webhook adapters handle strings that ordinary UTF-8 cannot represent exactly.',
 lambda l:'The '+l(CPP/'support/PrefsOracle.java','preferences oracle')+', '+l(CPP/'support/prefs.cpp','native harness')+' and '+l(CPP/'tools/prefs_differential.py','driver')+' enumerate all declared accessors, apply typed/null samples, inspect resulting JSON/wire data, exercise array aliases and malformed reads, and test names under English, Turkish, Azerbaijani, Lithuanian and Greek locales. The inspected '+l(CPP/'build/prefs-differential-results.json','report')+' has 1,130/1,130 matching cases; that total includes JsonDB and date-formatting cases. Exceptions are flattened to ERROR, so the suite does not establish exact error classes.',
 'Normalize “ A\tB ”: which characters disappear? Then explain why storing public-MDS false can still yield getPublicMDS true.',
 'Filesystem persistence failures, every retained-object alias and exact null error categories remain outside this accessor fixture. Password fields are ordinary JSON strings here; the lesson makes no claim of storage encryption. Partial counterpart, parity not_verified.')
author('src/org/minima/database/txpowdb/TxPoWDB.md','Coordinates the short-lived RAM mempool, longer-lived SQL TxPoW cache and on-chain transaction index.',[
 'Construct one RamDB and the two SQL stores. Loading opens the main file first, then an onchain sibling path. Save, hard-close and SQL-clean operations call the main store before the on-chain store.',
 'addTxPoW first gets the object ID and attempts to post a backup message containing the original object. Posting failures are caught/logged and insertion continues. Even duplicate additions attempt a fresh backup message.',
 'Insert into RAM only if that ID is absent. If SQL lacks the ID, resolve the wallet, evaluate TxPoW relevance and attempt SQL insertion. Return relevance, not a database-success flag. Existing SQL entries still trigger a fresh relevance calculation.',
 'addSQLTxPoW skips the RAM and backup-message paths and evaluates relevance only on a SQL miss.',
 'getTxPoW consults RAM first, then SQL on a miss. SQL retrieval is not automatically promoted into RAM. getAllTxPoW preserves request order and duplicates while omitting missing values.',
 'Child-block lookup obtains IDs from SQL and resolves each through the same RAM-first path. Mempool flags, coin-conflict search, cleanup and the complete map are delegated to RamDB.',
 'These operations are not one atomic transaction across all stores. A failure in relevance or SQL after RAM insertion can leave RAM changed; a retained backup message can observe subsequent mutation of its shared TxPoW.'
], 'C++ composes the same mapped stores, requires Main-message and wallet-resolution services and retains shared TxPoW/Message/RamData/map ownership. Optional identifiers allow null-key failure paths. File paths use the existing Java Unix helper and SQL clocks are injectable for comparison.',
 lambda l:'The '+l(CPP/'support/TxPoWDBOracle.java','Java owning-store oracle')+', '+l(CPP/'support/txpow_db.cpp','native harness')+' and '+l(CPP/'tools/txpow_db_differential.py','driver')+' execute actual RAM/SQL/searcher code with recorded JDBC and owner receivers. They compare duplicate objects, held map identities, mutable IDs, wallet short-circuit order, null/failing services, SQL faults, two-store lifecycle, cleanup and seeded sequences. Clocks are validated against operation windows before normalization. The inspected '+l(CPP/'build/txpow-db-results.json','report')+' has 1,355 programs, 7,407 operations and 80,960 matching recorded effects with no failures.',
 'If addTxPoW returns true after SQL insertion fails inside a caught SQL exception, what does true mean? Which fixture observations reveal whether RAM changed and a backup message was posted?',
 'JDBC and Main/wallet owners are fixture boundaries. Native H2 storage and the full node runtime remain incomplete, and NPE expression text/logger stacks are normalized. Return-value parity is therefore distinct from persistent-storage parity.')
